# 0032 libdrm (XeGfx Darwin stand-in): stale exported handles, ioctl tracing
#
# Base:  libdrm b97cbde15c5c3abfe44d78e8f57139e50f612fec (2.4.134) + NvGfx 5a19a73f gpu/userland/patches/
#        libdrm-macos.patch + libdrm-xegfx.patch (as gpu/userland/scripts/build_libdrm.sh applies them)
# Files: xf86drm_xegfx.c
# Apply: in the libdrm tree after the two NvGfx patches: patch -p1 < patches/0032-libdrm-stale-export-and-trace.patch
# Build: libdrm.2.dylib becdb50aa750 (after install_name_tool / ad-hoc signing), installed next to ANV in
#        /Library/GPUBundles/xemtl/
#
# Stale exported handles: the stand-in turns a dma-buf / sync_file / syncobj export into a pipe fd and keeps a table
# of (fd number, dev, ino) -> GEM handle or syncobj.  XNU gives a pipe the inode VM_KERNEL_ADDRHASH(pipe), and a pipe
# made right after one was closed often reuses both its number and its zone element, so the old entry kept matching
# and lookup() handed out the old export's handle.  On 11-04 a compressed scanout image was pinned as the IOSurface
# import that handle had come to name.  new_efd() now drops every entry with the number pipe() just returned before
# adding the new one, as well as entries the caller has closed since.
#
# Tracing (XEMTL_DRM_TRACE=1, set by the XeMTL first-loss journal of 0007): every ioctl routed through the stand-in is
# logged to syslog with route, minor, request, kernel return, result and errno (the first 512 successes and 64
# failures), and GEM_USERPTR / GEM_EXECBUFFER2 get detail probes (pointer, size, protection of the source pages; the
# buffer list and timeline fence extension) read with mach_vm_read_overwrite on the caller's own task, so a stale
# pointer cannot crash it.  Off by default.
#
--- a/xf86drm_xegfx.c
+++ b/xf86drm_xegfx.c
